id: walk-not-write-order
title: Why apply order is not your file order
widget type: param-playground
intent: Execution order comes from walking the graph, so file position is irrelevant, and concurrency changes the interleaving without ever violating an edge.
teaching note: Fix one graph of nine nodes: vpc -> {subnet_a, subnet_b, security_group}, subnet_a -> {instance_1, instance_2}, subnet_b -> instance_3, security_group -> {instance_1, instance_3}, and a standalone s3_bucket with no edges at all. Controls: a concurrency slider from 1 to 8, a dropdown of per-node durations (uniform 3s; vpc slow at 20s; instances slow at 15s), and a shuffle button that reorders the source file listing without touching the graph. Render two things live: a Gantt-style timeline of what runs when, and the source file listing beside it. Shuffling the file must visibly change nothing about the timeline, which is the whole point of the control. At concurrency 1 show a valid serial order and note that many different serial orders are equally valid. At higher concurrency show s3_bucket starting immediately alongside vpc despite sitting last in the file, and show the total time flattening out once concurrency exceeds the widest level of the graph, so the learner sees the critical path set by the edges rather than by the slider. Do not state any real default concurrency value; label the slider as the model's setting.

Function names used elsewhere in this lesson: _binding_state_for_step, grade_edges, plan_action, state_is_binding_init, state_is_binding_step, state_is_binding_view, three_way_grid_view

